# Demand for Grants Concerning Ministers, Deputy Ministers, Headquarters Staff, the State Legislature and Elections
Tamil title: அமைச்சர்கள், துணைத் தலைவர்கள், தலைமையிடப் பணியாளர்கள், மாநிலச் சட்டமன்றம், தேர்தல் ஆகியன பற்றிய நிதி ஒதுக்கீடு கோரிக்கை

Speech delivered on 16 March 1961.

**Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** Mr. Speaker, I suffered a great disappointment when differences of opinion arose during today's debate and the exchanges became heated. Only four days ago our Finance Minister said he had given up all his anger and resentment. I truly regret discovering that he cannot restrain his nature.

I point this out again only because a temperament that becomes angry so quickly will hinder him in the great responsibility he has assumed. When grants are being discussed and shortcomings are pointed out, he says: “You entered after accepting the Constitution and its arrangements. If you do not wish to act accordingly, go outside.”

This resembles the proprietor of a wholesale shop telling a clerk, because he dislikes the clerk's honesty: “Stay here if you will listen to me; otherwise step down and leave the shop.” A clerk who leaves in that manner will not remain silent. He will pick up earth from the middle of the street, throw it, and ask, “How long can this life last?” I humbly request that our Ministers, especially the Finance Minister, not conduct themselves in this fashion.

Some take pride in being inside; some take pride in remaining outside. Some, by entering, have submitted to certain rules and restrictions. The Finance Minister knows all this. Even so, I regret that he possesses an impulse toward sudden anger.

When my friend Anbazhagan said, “These Ministers are fit only to work in the Governor's residence,” the Minister became angry. From this I was proud to learn how lowly they themselves consider the Governor's office and service under the Governor.

Because anger arose and some harsh words were exchanged, nobody need conclude that democracy has truly come to a standstill. They disparaged the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam as engaging in false propaganda and spoke of it in degrading terms. A member from the Salem region said assaults had also occurred.

So far as I know, recent court proceedings in the cases filed against members of the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam in Salem do not support that general accusation. In the Ammapet case they were acquitted. In a police case arising from speeches at a public meeting in Victoria Market grounds, the court found fault on both sides, rather than only in the manner suggested here.

[Several lines concerning the Salem cases are damaged.]

When speaking of the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam, members not only accuse its people of behaving recklessly. One member said here that “they have become afraid because Ministers tour outside; therefore they criticise ministerial tours.”

For argument's sake, suppose we truly possess such fear. If you say that fear exists, you must also possess the hope that ministerial tours help your party grow. If that hope is real, you may accept that our fear is real. If you argue for the fear, you cannot deny the corresponding hope.

When ministerial tours are discussed, the problem is that Ministers are not actors on a stage who can sharply distinguish acts within official authority from acts within party limits. Actors may speak outside as elder brother, younger brother, uncle, brother-in-law, lover and beloved, then return backstage and address one another by their natural names. But one cannot draw a line saying Ministers perform official work from six to eight in the morning and party work from eight to ten. That is why their engagement in party propaganda during official tours was raised here as a criticism.

Our members say they do it out of fear. By replying that the Congress grows when Ministers speak and declines when all of you speak, you belittle yourselves. I regret that too.

I wish to discuss another matter briefly. Our Minister earlier referred to election expenditure. He said that there is neither defect nor offence when Ministers involve themselves in collecting money for it.

Since he has been an able criminal lawyer, it is cleverer to admit the act and argue that it is not an offence than to deny that it occurred. Some might simply deny collecting funds. Here, however, they say: “Yes, it happened. How is it an offence? Are we not party members and party leaders? What is wrong with Ministers collecting money for election expenditure?”

What is wrong with it? There are three kinds of wrong.

First, the public will willingly give money to the party they want only when they believe that party will fulfil their aspirations and that its policies accord with their own. If this is true, the party may publish an election appeal saying openly, “We have election expenses; please contribute.” That is the civilised method.

The hon. Finance Minister may ask me, “Is that how you act? Do you not tour place after place collecting money?” Such a question strengthens my argument. If, knowing that an appeal alone will not bring funds, Ministers travel from place to place to collect election money, they indirectly concede my claim that people give more when a Minister asks.

The influence of ministerial office must not be used to raise money for a party election fund. Some become Ministers because they possess influence; others gain influence because they become Ministers. However that influence arises, it should be used for the country's people. Using it to raise party election funds is an exceedingly degrading act.

I read in the newspapers that a Minister in Delhi—Srimathi Tarkeshwari Sinha, I believe—used her influence, toured many northern cities, and brought lakhs of rupees into the exchequer through the small-savings scheme. Let our Ministers likewise visit every market, climb the steps of house after house, visit mansions and wholesale shops, and use their influence for a public purpose. We will welcome it.

But I repeat again and again that using the influence arising from ministerial office to collect party election funds is an extremely degrading act. Only if our Ministers refrain from election fundraising hereafter will public suspicion concerning them diminish.

If they say, “No, we will continue doing it,” I lack both the political irresponsibility and the authority to tell them, as the Minister told us, “Then go outside.” I will not say that. But I can say with certainty that the time when the public tells them “Go outside” will come very soon.

I wish to tell the Minister one more thing. Anyone with a sense of political decency who reads the judgments of the Bombay and Calcutta High Courts concerning who contributes to the Congress election fund must feel disturbed.

Directors of the Tata company filed a petition in the Bombay High Court explaining, in substance, that they contributed to the Congress election fund because they believed that party had to remain in power to protect their corporate interests. This makes it starkly clear who gives money to the Congress and who wants to preserve the Congress.

[Part of the discussion of corporate contributors is damaged.]

Does the Congress possess no influence except through a small group of Ministers? If the hon. Thiru Subramaniam asks, people will contribute, but if Thiru Alagesan asks, will they not? Do you regard the distinguished leaders in each district as so lacking in influence that, while keeping one of them as Tamil Nadu Congress president, you intervene even in collecting election funds?

What are the district Congress leaders doing? What are the veteran Congress workers and sacrificers in those districts doing? Apart from eight of you, has nobody the standing to collect an election fund? Does “Congress” mean only you eight? Will people give to the Congress only if those eight ask? Why do you demean your party in this way?

I do not say you should not elevate yourselves. It was from that elevated position that Finance Minister Thiru Subramaniam said, “Remain if you wish; otherwise go.” Let that lofty quality grow. But must we conclude that Congress leaders lack even enough influence to collect an election fund? For these reasons, I humbly ask Ministers not to engage in collecting election funds.

There is another matter. My party has not officially advanced the proposal that Ministers should resign six months or at least three months before an election. Yet such an opinion is spreading rapidly in the country. You may argue that this is not practised in any democratic country. But in a democracy that has not yet fully matured, it may be one of the finest democratic qualities you can demonstrate to the people in order to protect democracy and your own good name.

[The end of this argument is damaged before the next subject begins.]

The respected Congress whip said that, on the question of the expenditure of nine lakh rupees on the Governor, we speak one way outside and another way inside. The Tamil word *korada* means an instrument that catches and pulls whatever it finds. He has seized upon something he found and pulled at it. At a suitable time and place I will answer him separately.

This time the whip caught hold of something that was not there and kept pulling it, like fighting with a hook in one's hand. In his haste he said that “the Tamil poet Naladiyar” had said something. There is no Tamil poet named Naladiyar; *Naladiyar* is the name of a book. I appreciate his enthusiasm, sympathise with his haste, and conclude my speech.

---NOTES---
The filename and context resolve the damaged header date “76.03.1961” as 16 March 1961. Several passages in Images 2-3, 4-5, 6-7 and 7-8 are degraded; only securely recoverable arguments are translated, with larger gaps marked. “Korada” is treated as the Tamil legislative term for party whip and also as the hook/pulling implement used in Anna's wordplay. The claims about company election contributions are translated as Anna's account of litigation, not independently verified fact. The Tamil source remains verbatim.
